- **Timestamped snapshot copy** — before deletion, `shutil.copy2(bathos.db, bathos.db.bak.YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS)` into the same `.bth/catalog/` dir; keep only the 3 most recent backups (prune on each new backup); recovery is a manual `cp` + rename
- **Content-addressed backup to `.bth/backups/`** — hash `bathos.db` with SHA256 before deletion; write to `.bth/backups/<sha256[:12]>.db` only if that hash doesn't already exist; zero disk waste on repeated force-rebuilds with identical DB; recovery via `bth compact --restore <sha256-prefix>`
- **`.bth/backups/latest.db` symlink** — always copy to a fixed path `latest.db` before deletion (no proliferation); one extra DB on disk at all times; recovery UX is obvious (`latest.db` is always the pre-last-rebuild state); pair with `bth compact --restore-last`
- **In-place rename to `.bak` with `--no-backup` escape hatch** — rename `bathos.db` → `bathos.db.bak` instead of deleting; rebuild writes fresh `bathos.db`; the `.bak` survives until the next `force_rebuild`; add `--no-backup` flag to skip the rename for scripts/CI that want clean state without disk overhead
- **WAL checkpoint + DuckDB `EXPORT DATABASE`** — before deletion, run DuckDB `EXPORT DATABASE '.bth/backups/YYYYMMDD/'` which writes portable CSV/Parquet per-table; survives schema migrations (can re-import into a newer schema); higher disk use but human-readable and diff-able; recovery requires `IMPORT DATABASE`
- **Dry-run preview gate** — no file backup; instead, `--force-rebuild` without `--yes` prints a diff of what warm-only data (postmortem annotations, output metadata) would be lost relative to cool tier and requires explicit confirmation; pairs with `--yes` for automation; preserves disk, forces awareness before destruction
